# Environment variables for the AI Agent

# Server
PORT=3001

# Web App API Configuration
# The agent communicates with the web app via API endpoints instead of direct database access
WEB_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Shared API Key for authentication (MUST match the web app's AGENT_API_KEY)

# The organisation this bot belongs to. Needed only for delivery receipts:
# an ack arrives from WhatsApp rather than from a request, so there is no
# per-call organisation to read it from. Find it in the app's database
# (`select id from organization`), or leave blank — the app looks receipts up
# by WhatsApp's own message id, which is globally unique.
AGENT_ORGANIZATION_ID=""
# Generate a strong random key: openssl rand -hex 32
AGENT_API_KEY="your-shared-agent-api-key-change-this-to-random-string"

# Mastra.ai Configuration
MASTRA_LOG_LEVEL="info"

# WhatsApp Configuration
# Set to "true" to enable WhatsApp integration (src/index.ts checks this
# exact string). WhatsApp requires Chrome/Chromium and launches it at boot —
# leave this unset/false to run as a plain chat/API service with no
# Chromium/Puppeteer involved at all.
ENABLE_WHATSAPP="false"

# Test mode - set to "true" to test without live WhatsApp connection
TEST_MODE="false"

# Path to a system Chromium binary, used instead of Puppeteer's own bundled
# download (see src/lib/whatsapp.ts, Linux only). The Dockerfile sets this
# automatically to /usr/bin/chromium — only needed here for a non-Docker
# Linux deploy with Chromium installed via apt/yum.
# PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH="/usr/bin/chromium"

# Where LocalAuth stores the bot's WhatsApp session so it survives restarts.
# Defaults to <cwd>/.wwebjs_auth; the Docker setups mount a volume at
# /app/.wwebjs_auth and set this explicitly — only customize if you run the
# agent outside Docker with a different data directory.
# WHATSAPP_AUTH_PATH=".wwebjs_auth"

# ---- WhatsApp assistant ----
# The assistant answers messages from the contacts an admin has added under
# Settings. It runs on OpenRouter so the model can be changed without a
# deploy. Get a key from https://openrouter.ai/keys
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=""
# Leave empty to use the model the code picks (see src/lib/model.ts, which
# explains the choice). Set this only to pin a different one — if the default
# id stops being valid, say, since OpenRouter renames models occasionally.
# The agent logs which id it is using at boot.
ASSISTANT_MODEL=""
# low | medium | high. These are reasoning models and their thinking is
# billed as output; "low" measured 7x cheaper and nearly twice as fast
# with no loss on the checks in agent/scripts/live-assistant-check.ts.
ASSISTANT_REASONING_EFFORT="low"
# How many past turns of a WhatsApp conversation the assistant is shown.
# It needs the previous message or two to follow "send me that as a pdf";
# beyond that the history just adds tokens to every message.
ASSISTANT_MEMORY_TURNS="10"
# Where the model is served from. Defaults to OpenRouter. Point it at a
# company gateway, a proxy, or a stub server when testing the assistant
# without spending tokens.
# ASSISTANT_BASE_URL="https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"

# ---- Removed ----
# OPENAI_API_KEY is gone. Both agents now run on OPENROUTER_API_KEY, so there
# is one provider and one key. It used to be read at import time and *thrown*
# on if missing, which took the whole service down on deploy.
#
# ADMIN_WHATSAPP_NUMBER and WHATSAPP_DEVELOPER_NUMBER_ONE/TWO are gone. Who may
# message the bot is managed under Settings -> WhatsApp assistant and stored in
# the database, so adding someone no longer needs a redeploy.

# ---- WhatsApp session storage ----
# The pairing is kept in Postgres so it survives redeploys. Without this the
# session lives in the container filesystem and every deploy demands a fresh
# QR scan. Use the same connection string as the app.
DATABASE_URL=""
